---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Metric from '../components/ui/Metric.astro';
import Callout from '../components/content/Callout.astro';

const businessArgs = [
  {
    n: '01',
    title: 'Agents increasingly choose the platform.',
    body:
      'A technical evaluator used to run the proof of concept personally. More and more, they ask an AI agent which product to use and have it build the trial. An agent that never picks your platform, or builds its own alternative, takes you out of an evaluation you never saw.',
  },
  {
    n: '02',
    title: 'Agent failures are invisible until you measure them.',
    body:
      'When an AI tool produces a broken integration, the developer may debug silently, find a workaround, or abandon the platform. The signal never reaches the product team, and company metrics look stable. An evaluation suite and agent traffic reporting surface the problem early.',
  },
  {
    n: '03',
    title: 'What you learn is kept, not carried in heads.',
    body:
      'The most valuable asset in a mature DevRel program is accumulated judgment about what a correct integration looks like. Today it lives with senior practitioners and leaves when they do. An evaluation suite of tasks and graders records it in a form that outlasts both staff changes and changes of tooling.',
  },
  {
    n: '04',
    title: 'Evidence makes the engineering case.',
    body:
      'When DevRel can show where agents lose time on the platform and where developers have to step in, with traces and failure cases attached, a request to engineering becomes a product investment argument.',
  },
];
---

<PageLayout
  title="Agentic Developer Relations for Organizations"
  description="The business case for Agentic Developer Relations — four arguments, what the data says, investment by organization size, and the scorecard to report to leadership."
>
  <section class="container-site pt-16 pb-4">
    <p class="eyebrow mb-4">For companies</p>
    <h1 class="max-w-3xl">Agentic Developer Relations for Organizations Evaluating DevRel Investment</h1>
    <p class="lead text-muted mt-5 max-w-2xl">
      The business case — four arguments, what the data says, investment
      by organization size, and the scorecard that makes agent experience
      visible to leadership.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container-prose pb-12">
    <h2>The question under all the others</h2>
    <p class="mt-5">
      Every executive evaluating DevRel investment has one question beneath
      all the others: <em>does DevRel drive revenue?</em>
    </p>
    <p class="mt-5">
      The traditional answer involves reach, engagement, pipeline influence,
      and brand sentiment — metrics that are real but notoriously difficult
      to connect to adoption numbers. DevRel teams have spent years
      developing increasingly sophisticated attribution models to answer a
      question that the metrics were never designed to answer directly.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-5">
      Agentic Developer Relations changes this.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-5">
      The First-Attempt Integration Success Rate measures something
      closer to adoption than any of those: whether a developer's
      AI-assisted integration works. The hypothesis is simple. Developers
      whose integrations succeed tend to adopt. Developers who meet
      systematic AI-generated integration failures tend to choose
      alternatives — silently, without generating the support tickets or
      community posts that would give the company a chance to respond.
      The rate is measurable today. Its link to adoption is something to
      test against your own activation data.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-5">
      Agentic Developer Relations is the practice of measuring that rate, along
      with what agents choose and what success costs, and improving them.
      It gives a DevRel program a way to test the executive question
      with data, not proxies.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container-site">
      <div class="container-prose px-0 mb-6">
        <h2>What the data says</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-5 max-w-4xl mx-auto mb-8">
        <Metric value="19%" label="Slower with AI in METR's early-2025 trial" deltaDirection="down" />
        <Metric value="1.36 → 2.9" label="PRs per engineer, 0% vs. 100% AI adoption (Jellyfish)" deltaDirection="up" />
        <Metric value="1.25×" label="Most a faster model can gain if 80% of the time is tool overhead" />
      </div>

      <div class="container-prose px-0">
        <p>
          In <a href="https://metr.org/blog/2025-07-10-early-2025-ai-experienced-os-dev-study/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">METR's early-2025 randomized controlled trial</a>,
          experienced developers took <strong>19% longer</strong> with AI
          tools while believing they were about <strong>20% faster</strong>.
          <a href="https://metr.org/blog/2026-02-24-uplift-update/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">METR's 2026 follow-up</a> leans towards a speedup, on data
          METR itself calls weak. <a href="https://jellyfish.co/blog/jellyfish-openai-team-up-to-measure-impact-of-ai-coding-tools/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jellyfish</a> found that companies
          at 100% AI adoption merged <strong>2.90</strong> pull requests
          per engineer against <strong>1.36</strong> at 0% — an
          association, not a cause.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5">
          The productivity evidence is unsettled, and developers' own
          impressions are a poor guide. The arithmetic is not in doubt: if
          80% of the time an agent spends on your platform is tool
          overhead, a better model can speed the whole up by at most 1.25×.
        </p>
        <Callout type="primary" label="The executive frame">
          Your
          documentation, your API surface, your auth flows, your error model,
          and your integration examples are the environment for every
          developer integrating with your platform using AI tools.
          Whether that environment helps or hinders is something you can
          measure.
          <strong>Agentic Developer Relations is the discipline of managing that
          environment deliberately.</strong>
        </Callout>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container-site">
    <div class="container-prose px-0 mb-10">
      <h2>Four business arguments</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 max-w-5xl mx-auto">
      {
        businessArgs.map((a) => (
          <div class="border border-border rounded-xl bg-surface p-6">
            <p class="font-mono text-primary font-bold text-sm mb-2">{a.n}</p>
            <h3 class="text-heading text-lg font-bold mb-3 leading-snug">{a.title}</h3>
            <p class="text-body text-sm leading-relaxed">{a.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container-site">
      <div class="container-prose px-0 mb-6">
        <h2>Investment tiers</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-primary-surface">
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Organization</th>
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Minimum viable</th>
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Full program</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Small (1–5 DevRel)</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">One practitioner owns this one day per week. A hand-run suite of 10 tasks. Agent-facing guidance kept in the docs.</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Grow and automate the suite. Make the role full-time when your own data justifies it: the agent share of your docs traffic, and the gap your first runs reveal.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Mid-size (6–15)</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">A full-time DevRel lead for agent experience. Automated suite across several models. Each agent-facing artifact tested.</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Selection monitoring. Suite runs on every release. A named counterpart in docs and in platform engineering.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Large (15+)</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">A small DevRel group working with an Agent Experience or platform team. The scorecard reported alongside community measures.</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">A published evaluation suite. Security review of agent-facing content. Runtime controls for agents that operate the product.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container-site">
    <div class="container-prose px-0">
      <h2>The scorecard for leadership</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-6">
        <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-primary-surface">
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Metric</th>
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">What it measures</th>
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Why it matters</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Selection rate</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">How often agents choose the platform for a neutral request, and how often they build their own</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Whether you are in the evaluation at all</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Completion rate (FAISR)</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Share of agent runs that reach a working integration with no human help</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Proposed leading indicator of AI-assisted adoption; validate against your own activation data</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Cost</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Turns, tokens, and time per run, including failures</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">What success costs your developers</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Artifact lift</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">The measured effect of each thing published for agents</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Whether the program's output is working</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Agent reach</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Agent share of documentation traffic; how often published skills and tools are used</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">How much of your audience now arrives through an agent</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Outcome link</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Activation and retention after a staged change</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">The test of whether the rest matters</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container-prose">
      <h2>The question to ask</h2>
      <p class="lead mt-5">
        When a developer asks an AI coding tool to integrate with your
        platform, does it work?
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        If you don't know the answer, your DevRel program is not measuring
        the most consequential dimension of your developer experience.
        Agentic Developer Relations is the discipline that makes the answer knowable —
        and then systematically improves it.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-8">
        <a
          href="/about/#contact"
          class="inline-flex items-center px-6 py-3 font-semibold rounded-md bg-primary text-white hover:bg-primary-light no-underline"
        >
          Get in touch about speaking or advisory work →
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</PageLayout>
